  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale:\nThe central idea is to combine multi-resolution optimization with smooth function approximation to overcome the limitations of standard gradient-based optimization methods in escaping local minima. Traditional optimization methods often get trapped in suboptimal regions because they cannot adequately handle the discrete nature of the problem. This approach uses hierarchical grids to progressively refine solutions and smooth approximations to guide the search towards globally optimal regions. We assume that the optimal solution exhibits self-similar structure across scales, allowing us to transfer information between resolutions. The method allows for dynamic adjustment of resolution and uses exponential smoothing to balance exploration and exploitation. Key benefits include: (1) faster convergence by leveraging global information, (2) improved robustness to initialization, and (3) direct targeting of critical regions through adaptive smoothing.\n\nMathematical Specification:\n1. Representation: h(x) is discretized over a grid of n points, with \u0394x = 2/n. Values are in [0,1] and satisfy \u222b h(x) dx = 1.\n2. Objective: Minimize C_5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n3. Smoothed Objective: f_\u03b1(h) = (1/\u03b1) log(\u2211\u2096 exp(\u03b1(c\u2096 - max(c\u2096))) + max(c\u2096), where c\u2096 is the correlation score at lag k.\n4. Optimization Domain: The function space is represented by its grid vector h \u2208 \u211d\u207f with bounds [0,1]. Gradient is given by:\n   \u2207f_\u03b1(h) = (1/\u03b1) \u2211_{k} [w\u2096 dx] [sign(clip(c\u2096 - max(c\u2096), -\u03b1, \u221e)] \n   where w\u2096 = exp(\u03b1(c\u2096 - max(c\u2096))) / \u03a3\u2c7c exp(\u03b1(c\u2c7c - max(c\u2c7c)))\n5. Constraint Handling: Use a combination of projection and penalty terms: p(h) = \u03bc(q(h))\u00b2, where q(h) = \u222b h(x) dx - 1.\n\nImplementation Procedure:\n1. Initialization (Step 1):\n   - If initial_h_values provided, project to feasible set (clip to [0,1] and adjust sum). \n   - Else, generate random start with uniform adjustment to meet sum constraint.\n\n2. Multi-resolution Cycle:\n   - Start with coarse grid (n=64) or parent grid (n=500 if available)\n   - For each resolution level:\n        a. Interpolate previous best candidate (linear projection)\n        b. Smoothed objective initialization:\n            - Use temperature decay schedule: \u03b1\u2080 = 100000, \u03c4 = 200 (initial temp steps)\n        c. Run multi-stage optimization:\n            - Stage 1: Global search (NSGA-III) with grid rotation\n            - Stage 2: Local refinement (Gradient-based with adaptive momentum)\n            - Stage 3: Top-shift focus (gradient boosting on top lags)\n\n3. Objective Evaluation:\n   - Use exact integration with \u0394x weight\n   - Handle boundary conditions by clamping shifts outside domain\n   - Compute full correlation vector for all lags\n\n4. Transition Between Resolutions:\n   - From n to m = nr * n: h_coarse \u2264 h_fine \u2264 h_coarse (to maintain local structure)\n   - Maintain top candidates between levels\n   - Compute score at original resolution for final validation\n\n5. Parameter Tuning:\n   - Use Bayesian optimization for temperature selection\n   - Maintain dim_ratio = h_std / (sqrt(n) * \u03bc) for penalty balance\n   - For grid interpolation: use cubic spline with adaptive refinement\n\nSearch Choices and Variations:\n1. Dimensionality Control:\n   - Default: Linear interpolation for refinement\n   - Variation: Wavelet-based transfer (requires pyWavelets)\n   - Variation: Fourier feature mapping (increase effective dimension)\n\n2. Initial Projection:\n   - Default: Simple clipping and adjustment\n   - Variation: Clipped expectation adjustment:\n     s = np.clip(h, 0, 1)\n     diff = target_sum - np.sum(s)\n     adjustment = np.clip(diff * mask, -0.03, 0.03)\n     h += adjustment\n   - Variation: Piecewise uniform segmentation with random split points\n\n3. Optimization Methods:\n   - Default: Exponential smoothing gradient descent\n   - Variation: Stabilized Adam: m_t = \u03b21 * m_{t-1} + (1-\u03b21) * \u2207f(h), with adaptive learning\n   - Variation: Dimension polishing with SVD projection\n\n4. Quantum-Inspired Search:\n   - Variation (may exceed budget): Use population annealing with quantum operators\n   - Variation (safe): Add simulated tempering factor periodically\n\nCompute Budget and Robustness:\n1. Time Allocation:\n   - Coarse (32-128 points): 20% budget (220s)\n   - Medium (129-1024 points): 50% budget (550s)\n   - Fine (1025+ points): 30% budget (330s) [capped at 1024]\n   - Fallback: Fixed 100s at parent resolution if stuck\n\n2. Early Termination:\n   - Check every 5 optimization steps\n   - If improvement < 1e-6*best_score for 500s, activate fallback\n   - If gradient contamination (|gradient| > 1e5) for 3 consecutive steps, restart with coarsening\n\n3. Fallback Strategy:\n   - Maintain parent-resolution solution\n   - Correct deviation using: h = h0 + progress * (h_best - h0) with progress = 0.3\n   - Apply dimensionality reduction: n = min(500, n) or use default parent\n\n4. Numerical Safeguards:\n   - Handle NaN/Inf: Use finite bounds and early termination\n   - Warm start triggered if (projection steps required) > 3\n   - Execution limit: Integrate based on \u03c3(h) scaling with n\n\nValidation and Return Contract:\n1. Final Check:\n   - Verify |sum(h)*\u0394x - 1| < 1e-6\n   - Check all h[i] \u2208 [0,1] via clamping\n   - Reevaluate C_5 on original grid\n\n2. Performance Monitoring:\n   - Run at least 3 levels to validate improvement\n   - Maintain rolling best-10 scores\n   - Violation detection (over 0.38 limit) \u2192 fallback\n\n3. Return Criteria:\n   - Final candidate C_5 \u2264 0.3808\n   - Stable improvement over projection steps\n   - Break conditions: time expired or candidate stabilized\n\n4. Special Checks:\n   - Zero-sum guard: Use \u222b h(x) dx = 1/(2\u0394x)\n   - NaN detection: Replace all |candidate score| = 0 or inf with fallback\n   - Lag outlier check: Remove extreme lags before max computation if <300 valid shifts\n\nThe rollout has been implemented with a focus on logarithmic performance scaling. The algorithm is designed to run within 1100 seconds through adaptive dimension management and smart resource allocation.",
